  "narrative": {
    "intro": "Texas received $82.9B in federal contract obligations in FY2026 to date. The state recorded 168,852 awards with an average award size of $491K. Obligations fell 3.9% year over year. Fiscal-year totals show growth and volatility: FY21 totaled $55.1B, FY22 totaled $67.6B, FY23 totaled $81.9B, FY24 totaled $59.3B, and FY25 totaled $86.4B. The Department of Defense, Department of Homeland Security, and Department of Veterans Affairs are the largest buying agencies by obligation in FY2026 to date. Commercial and Institutional Building Construction, Aircraft Manufacturing, and Pharmaceutical Preparation Manufacturing are the largest industries by obligation in the same period. MCKESSON CORPORATION, LOCKHEED MARTIN CORPORATION, and FISHER SAND & GRAVEL CO are the largest vendors by obligation in FY2026 to date. Figures presented are contract obligations and current-year totals are partial, fiscal year to date.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "Which agencies drive federal contracting in Texas?",
        "body": "Department of Defense obligated $36.2B in FY2026 to date. Department of Homeland Security obligated $25.1B and Department of Veterans Affairs obligated $16.2B. Department of Energy, National Aeronautics and Space Administration, and the other top buyers together account for the remaining obligations listed for FY2026 to date."
      },
      {
        "heading": "What industries and vendors dominate spending in Texas?",
        "body": "Commercial and Institutional Building Construction obligated $17.6B, Aircraft Manufacturing obligated $12.2B, and Pharmaceutical Preparation Manufacturing obligated $11B in FY2026 to date. MCKESSON CORPORATION obligated $11B, LOCKHEED MARTIN CORPORATION obligated $10.8B and LOCKHEED MARTIN CORPORATION obligated $9.4B among the largest vendors by obligation. Other large industry and vendor obligations include Guided Missile and Space Vehicle Manufacturing at $10.3B and FISHER SAND & GRAVEL CO at $4.6B."
      }
    ]
  },
